A. Statement changes
A1. Theorem 6 is false for $m \ge 2$; it holds for $m = 1$
Theorem 6 claims, for all $m$,
$$|S^m_{\mathrm{cont},1}f|{\mathbf{L}^2(\mathbb{R}^m+)} \le C_m |f|_{\mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)}.$$
For $m \ge 2$ the left-hand side is $+\infty$ for essentially every $f \ne 0$, so no constant works.
Where the proof fails. The induction step replaces
$$G\big(U_{\lambda_m}\cdots U_{\lambda_1}f\big) \quad\text{by}\quad G\big(\mathcal{W}{\lambda_m}U{\lambda_{m-1}}\cdots U_{\lambda_1}f\big)$$
"since the $G$ function has a modulus already", and then applies the $\mathbf{H}^1 \to \mathbf{L}^1$ bound (32) to the second expression. The quantity actually being bounded is $|G(g)|1$ with $g = U{\lambda_m}\cdots U_{\lambda_1}f$. That $g$ is nonnegative and, unless it vanishes identically, has strictly positive integral, so it is never in $\mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$: every element of $\mathbf{H}^1$ has zero average. Cancellation of the wavelet is available at the first layer only.
Why the bound itself is false. Fix $(\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_{m-1})$ with $g = U_{\lambda_{m-1}}\cdots U_{\lambda_1}f \ne 0$. Since $g \ge 0$ and $g \in \mathbf{L}^1$, $\hat g$ is continuous with $\hat g(0) = |g|1 > 0$. Choosing $\omega_0$ with $\hat\psi(\omega_0) \ne 0$, and using $|h|1 \ge |\hat h|\infty$ together with $\widehat{\psi\lambda}(\omega) = \lambda^{n/2}\hat\psi(\lambda\omega)$,
$$|g*\psi_\lambda|_1 ;\ge; \lambda^{n/2},|\hat g(\omega_0/\lambda)|,|\hat\psi(\omega_0)| ;\ge; \tfrac12 \lambda^{n/2}|g|_1 |\hat\psi(\omega_0)|$$
for all large $\lambda$, while $\int^\infty \lambda^{n},d\lambda/\lambda^{n+1} = \infty$. The same computation applies to the dyadic transform, where $|g*\psi_j|_1 \to |g|_1|\psi|_1$ as $j \to \infty$.
The hypothesis "$g \ne 0$ on a set of positive measure" holds whenever $f * \psi_{\lambda_1} \not\equiv 0$ for $\lambda_1$ in a set of positive measure, hence for every $f \ne 0$ when $\hat\psi$ vanishes identically on no ray.
Corrected statement. Theorem 6 holds for $m = 1$, and its proof needs only (24)–(25). In particular Lemmas 4 and 5 are not needed for it; they enter only through Corollary 11 and Corollary 14.
A2. Corollary 7 also holds only for $m = 1$
Analyticity of $\psi$ gives $\mathcal{W}\lambda h \in \mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R})$ for every $h \in \mathbf{L}^1(\mathbb{R})$, with $|\mathcal{W}\lambda h|{\mathbf{H}^1} = 2|\mathcal{W}\lambda h|1$, but the next layer receives $|\mathcal{W}\lambda h| \ge 0$. The divergence in A1 applies verbatim.
A3. Replacement for $m \ge 2$: a damped scale measure
For $\varepsilon > 0$ put $\theta_\varepsilon = \sqrt{2/\varepsilon}$ and
$$\omega_\varepsilon(s,t) = \Big(\tfrac{\min(s,t)}{\max(s,t)}\Big)^{\varepsilon}, \qquad \Omega^m_\varepsilon(\lambda) = \prod_{k=2}^m \omega_\varepsilon(\lambda_{k-1},\lambda_k),$$
$$|F|^2_{\mathbf{L}^2_\varepsilon(\mathbb{R}^m_+)} = \int_0^\infty!!\cdots!\int_0^\infty |F|^2,\Omega^m_\varepsilon,\frac{d\lambda_1}{\lambda_1^{n+1}}\cdots\frac{d\lambda_m}{\lambda_m^{n+1}},$$
with $2^{-\varepsilon|j_k-j_{k-1}|}$ in the dyadic case. For $m = 1$ this is the original norm. Then for every $m \ge 1$, every $\varepsilon > 0$ and every $f \in \mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$,
$$|S^m_{\mathrm{cont},1}f|{\mathbf{L}^2\varepsilon(\mathbb{R}^m_+)} ;\le; (|\psi|1\theta\varepsilon)^{m-1}, C_1 |f|_{\mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)},$$
where $C_1$ is the $m = 1$ constant. The proof is Young's inequality at each layer past the first, plus Theorem 6 at the first, using $\lambda_j^{n},d\lambda_j/\lambda_j^{n+1} = d\lambda_j/\lambda_j$ and $\int_0^\infty \omega_\varepsilon(s,t),dt/t = 2/\varepsilon$.
Damping discards only the coarse outer scales $\lambda_k \gg \lambda_{k-1}$, where $|g*\psi_{\lambda_k}|_1 \approx \lambda_k^{n/2}|\psi|1|g|1$ merely reproduces the previous layer's coefficient. Restricting instead to $R^{-1} \le \lambda_k/\lambda{k-1} \le R$ gives the same bounds with $\theta\varepsilon^2$ replaced by $2\log R$, which is closer to what implementations do.
A4. Statements inheriting A1
Corollary 14 ($q = 1$ dilation stability, stated without proof): for $m \ge 2$ both sides must be measured in the damped norm; for $m = 1$ it is unchanged. A proof is now supplied. With $\psi^c := (1-c)^{-n/2}\psi_{(1-c)} = \psi + \Psi$, Lemma 9 reads $\mathcal{W}\lambda L\tau = L_\tau \mathcal{W}^c_\lambda$, hence $V_k L_\tau = L_\tau V^c_k$ and
$$S^m_{\mathrm{cont},1}L_\tau f = (1-c)^{-n} S^{m,c}{\mathrm{cont},1} f.$$
One then telescopes layer by layer, using that the modulus is nonexpansive on $\mathbf{L}^1$; each error term is $|(V^c{k-1}f)*\Psi_{\lambda_k}|_1$, and $|\Psi|_1 = O(|c|)$ by Lemma 10.
Lemma 23 and Lemma 26, $q = 1$ bullets: the domain is $\mathbf{H}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$, not $\mathbf{L}^1(\mathbb{R}^n)$, and for $m \ge 2$ the target is the damped norm.
Theorem 24 and Theorem 27, $q = 1$ parts: damped norms for $m \ge 2$.
